WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
 Okay, well as you can see, waiting in the wings there, is our next inductor.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:11.000
 The Irish American community is so proud to have Jerry Quinn as part of it.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:17.680
 He's a 2012 inductee of the Hall of Fame. He's been on the air during Irish

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.000
 radio for 30 plus years.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.000
 30 years!

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:28.000
 [applause]

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:35.170
 He's a founder of the Cleveland Irish American Club Eastside, which is special

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:36.000
 to me.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.810
 And in 1970, he did that in 1978, and he co-founded the Mayo Society here in

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.000
 Cleveland in 2004.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.120
 Even those of us that are not from County Mayo will appreciate what a great

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.000
 thing that is.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.000
 And you might want to check out the Mayo Hall. It's a wonderful event.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.000
 It's my pleasure to introduce to you Mr. Jerry Quinn.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.000
 [applause]

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:10.480
 First let me say thank you Debbie and Dan Hanson for following this wonderful

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.000
 organization.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.000
 I've been here every year since it was founded, and I was very honored to be

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.000
 the inductor of the Hall of Fame

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:25.360
 in 2011. The Mayo Society, it wasn't slight correction. It's 20 years old

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:32.000
 actually, but we became a 501(c)(3) in 2004.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:38.370
 You know, Debbie said I was 30 years on the radio. We're actually stuck our 39th

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:39.000
 year.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:45.180
 So we're getting over to some of us. I don't know if I have any of the original

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.000
 listeners left or not.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:54.000
 It's a joy to do. And a lot of people say to me often, "Do you ever get nervous?"

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
 I said, "I do." Especially when I'm speaking public like this to 500 people.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.000
 They've been on the radio for 39 years. That's a little different.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:11.000
 Television of course is much easier. Chris wants to head it back.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:20.110
 Anyway, it reminds me of a young priest who was very nervous about giving his

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:21.000
 first sermon.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:28.090
 So he said to the old pastor, he said, "You know, I'm so nervous. Next Sunday I

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.000
 have to do my first sermon,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.050
 and the bishop is going to be in the congregation. I don't know what we're

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.000
 going to do."

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.010
 But the priest says, "It's very simple. All you have to do is to get the

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:41.000
 attention of the congregation,

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.000
 and it's plain sailing from there." He says, "Watch me."

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.000
 So the old priest got up to give his sermon. First thing he said was,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:57.000
 "Some of my seniors of my life were spent in the arms of another man's wife.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.000
 It was dead silence, just like it is here in college."

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:09.000
 Then he paused and went on to say, "Of course, that woman was my own brother."

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.000
 He was a sigh of relief.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.190
 So anyway, the young curate saw that and said, "Not that sport, folks. I'm

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:18.000
 going to try it next Sunday."

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
 One of them suddenly came. He started off, "Some of the best years of my life

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.000
 were spent in the arms of another man's wife."

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.190
 And he looked out and he saw the bishop and he froze. He couldn't think of

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:34.000
 anything.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.570
 His mind was completely blank. And after what seemed like a long time, he broke

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:40.000
 it out,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.000
 and right now I can't remember her name.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.000
 And what was it?

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.000
 We're here this evening to understand what Northeast Ohio's finance citizens

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:00.000
 are.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
 I am privileged to be asked for our post to introduce one of these honorees.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.990
 Now I'm not going to repeat to you what's in the program, because I'm sure all

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:12.000
 of you have read it.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.000
 But I want to tell you about a personal story.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:22.000
 I'm talking about the first time that I met Sheila Murphy Crawford.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:28.000
 It was the summer of 1961, a long time ago.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:35.000
 I had just arrived from Ireland. I was being ashore of the city by my cousins.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.920
 But what do you think they took me? First place, they took me to the Irish

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:44.000
 cultural garden.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:51.000
 And there they introduced me to the Murphy family, who happened to be working

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.000
 there.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:59.000
 We ate lantern flowers and doing what the Murphy family are still doing today

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.000
 in the garden.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.000
 And how was Sheila, her mom, her dad, her sister, and her brother?

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:15.300
 Now it is easy to see how the great parental influence shaped the person that

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:19.000
 we see here we are honoring here tonight.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:25.000
 Now I think you all should have read the program. Sheila is a school teacher.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.000
 And I've had all my radio programs several times.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:35.990
 And we talk about education, and we talk about English, and we talk about Irish

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:37.000
 poetry.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:42.160
 And one of our favorite pieces of poetry, and mine, just had to be learned, is

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:50.000
 something that was written by William Butler Eats way back in 1906.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.870
 And it's called The Cloths of Heaven. I don't know if any of you have ever

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:55.000
 heard it or not.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:02.000
 And it goes like this.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:11.110
 Had I the heavens and broader clots, and wrought with gold and silver light,

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:15.660
 the blue, the dandy, and the dark clots of night and the light and the half

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:16.000
 light,

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:25.000
 I would spread the clots under your feet, but I didn't pour upon you my dreams.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.410
 I would spread my dreams under your feet, walk softly, because you've driven my

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:30.000
 dreams.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.370
 As Sheila had my favorite poem, I said it many times on the radio, she loved it,

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:38.000
 she loved it, and what it creates.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:43.920
 But the other thing about her is, this is a lady that is always giving, always

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:48.000
 organizing, always caring about our community.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.000
 We value her commitment to the Irish community.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:58.740
 Now all of us know that her value, her value of friendship, and good will is

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:02.000
 the one thing that we value most.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.000
 Sheila, thank you for your friendship.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:10.570
 Thank you for all that you do for the Irish community. We love you dearly,

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:13.000
 every one of us. Thank you.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:46.000
 [applause]

